('b441e222-df5f-5b26-b7a4-671bbfa3925d', '50ff4bc9-26e6-445a-abf4-2137ff76c6eb', 'c32a7123-0b11-417e-a3dd-2024f9fdf5d2', 'a-level', 'P2.1', '9702 coursebook P2.1 · Planning and analysis', 41, 41, 'A student records x = 1.0, 2.0, 4.0, 8.0 and y = 3.0, 8.5, 24, 68 and suspects y = kxⁿ. (a) Explain why a graph of lg y against lg x is useful. (b) Use the first and last pairs of values to find n.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1.501', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) lg y = n lg x + lg k, a straight line of gradient n. (b) n = (lg 68 − lg 3.0) ÷ (lg 8.0 − lg 1.0) = 1.50. Mark scheme (5 marks). B1: lg y = n lg x + lg k. B1: gradient gives n. M1: logs of first and last points. C1: substitution. A1: n = 1.50.', 5, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 400 410" role="img" aria-labelledby="fg9fc959d1-t fg9fc959d1-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fg9fc959d1-t">Readings of y against x</title><desc id="fg9fc959d1-d">Table and graph of y against x: (1.0, 3.0), (2.0, 8.5), (4.0, 24), (8.0, 68); the points lie on a curve that bends upwards.</desc><g transform="translate(0,0)"><rect x="156.1" y="8" width="87.8" height="140" rx="0" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><rect x="156.1" y="8" width="87.8" height="28" rx="0" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="178.1" y="22" font-size="14" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor;font-weight:600">x</text><text x="221.9" y="22" font-size="14" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor;font-weight:600">y</text><line x1="200" y1="8" x2="200" y2="148" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="156.1" y1="36" x2="243.