('b4049ba9-7028-596a-bd6f-75c1d796ecd4', '50ff4bc9-26e6-445a-abf4-2137ff76c6eb', 'c32a7123-0b11-417e-a3dd-2024f9fdf5d2', 'a-level', 'P2.1', '9702 coursebook P2.1 · Planning and analysis', 44, 44, 'A capacitor discharges through a 47 kΩ resistor, so V = V₀e^(−t/RC). A graph of ln(V/V) against t/s has gradient −0.050 s⁻¹. (a) Write ln V in terms of t. (b) Calculate RC. (c) Calculate C in µF.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '425.5', 'µF', false, NULL, false, ARRAY['4.255e-4']::text[], '(a) ln V = ln V₀ − t/RC. (b) RC = 1 ÷ 0.050 = 20 s. (c) C = 20 ÷ 47 × 10³ = 4.255 × 10⁻⁴ F. In µF: 4.255 × 10⁻⁴ × 10⁶ = 426. Mark scheme (6 marks). B1: ln V linear in t. C1: gradient = −1/RC. C1: RC = 20 s. M1: C = RC/R. C1: µF. A1: C = 426 µF.', 6, NULL, NULL),
('5a5e9c71-be1e-54eb-9f60-af4f8cc7d49f', '50ff4bc9-26e6-445a-abf4-2137ff76c6eb', 'c32a7123-0b11-417e-a3dd-2024f9fdf5d2', 'a-level', 'P2.1', '9702 coursebook P2.1 · Planning and analysis', 45, 45, 'A plan is needed to find how the terminal speed v of a steel sphere falling through oil depends on its radius r. Which plan is best?', NULL, 3, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'Use spheres of several radii (measured with a micrometer), time each between two marks low in the tube after checking equal times for equal distances, keep the oil at constant temperature, and plot lg v against lg r', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Only the radius should change; terminal speed must be measured where it is constant; viscosity depends on temperature. Mark scheme (5 marks). C1: vary r, measure with micrometer. C1: speed measured after terminal speed reached. C1: control temperature of oil. C1: log–log analysis. A1: D (Use spheres of several radii (measured with a micrometer), time each between two marks low in the tube after checking equal times for equal distances, keep the oil at constant temperature, and plot lg v against lg r).', 5, NULL, NULL),
('d5d4c349-f122-51a2-b81f-00e6a284971d', '50ff4bc9-26e6-445a-abf4-2137ff76c6eb', 'c32a7123-0b11-417e-a3dd-2024f9fdf5d2', 'a-level', 'P2.1', '9702 coursebook P2.1 · Planning and analysis', 46, 46, 'For a stretched string, f = (1/2L)√(T/µ). With L = 0.80 m, a graph of f against √T has gradient 6.5 Hz N^(−1/2). (a) Express the gradient in terms of L and µ. (b) Calculate µ, in units of 10⁻³ kg m⁻¹.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '9.246', '10⁻³ kg m⁻¹', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) gradient = 1/(2L√µ). (b) √µ = 1 ÷ (2 × 0.80 × 6.5) = 0.09615, so µ = (0.09615)² = 0.00925 kg m⁻¹. Mark scheme (5 marks). B1: gradient = 1/(2L√µ). M1: rearranged for √µ. C1: substitution. C1: square. A1: µ = 9.25 × 10⁻³ kg m⁻¹.', 5, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 400 290" role="img" aria-labelledby="fgab62a235-t fgab62a235-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fgab62a235-t">f against √T</title><desc id="fgab62a235-d">A straight line through the origin on a graph of frequency f against √T, gradient 6.5 Hz N^(−1/2).</desc><line x1="29.4" y1="34" x2="29.4" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="100" y1="34" x2="100" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="170.5" y1="34" x2="170.5" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="241" y1="34" x2="241" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="311.5" y1="34" x2="311.5" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="382" y1="34" x2="382" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width