('be222721-59d4-51d8-9da5-8024f7598535', '50ff4bc9-26e6-445a-abf4-2137ff76c6eb', 'c32a7123-0b11-417e-a3dd-2024f9fdf5d2', 'a-level', 'P2.1', '9702 coursebook P2.1 · Planning and analysis', 49, 49, 'The range of a projectile launched at a fixed angle θ from ground level is R = v²sin 2θ/g. A graph of R against v² has gradient 0.085 s² m⁻¹. (a) State the gradient in terms of θ and g. (b) Calculate θ, the smaller possible angle. Take g = 9.81 m s⁻².', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '28.25', '°', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) gradient = sin 2θ/g. (b) sin 2θ = 0.085 × 9.81 = 0.8339, so θ = sin⁻¹(0.085 × 9.81) ÷ 2 = 28.2°. Mark scheme (5 marks). B1: gradient = sin 2θ/g. M1: sin 2θ = gradient × g. C1: inverse sine. C1: halve. A1: θ = 28.2°.', 5, NULL, NULL),
('2cf868dd-54e8-55ac-98b4-d32a724e94a7', '50ff4bc9-26e6-445a-abf4-2137ff76c6eb', 'c32a7123-0b11-417e-a3dd-2024f9fdf5d2', 'a-level', 'P2.1', '9702 coursebook P2.1 · Planning and analysis', 50, 50, 'A student finds the time t for a trolley to run down a ramp of length 1.20 m for several heights h and plots 1/t² against h. Theory gives 1/t² = gh/(2s²) for a ramp of length s, ignoring friction. (a) State the expected gradient in terms of g and s. (b) Calculate the expected gradient, in s⁻² m⁻¹. Take g = 9.81 m s⁻².', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '3.406', 's⁻² m⁻¹', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) gradient = g/(2s²). (b) Gradient = 9.81 ÷ (2 × 1.20²) = 3.41 s⁻² m⁻¹. Mark scheme (5 marks). B1: gradient = g/2s². M1: substitution. C1: s squared. C1: value. A1: gradient = 3.41 s⁻² m⁻¹.', 5, NULL, NULL)
